WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.839
Hun, I think someone’s stealing our trashcans.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.260
Oh no, that’s just the guy from Curbit.com.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:14.440
It’s an online service that rolls your cans
to the curb on garbage day.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.360
Only $14.99 a month!

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.130
That seems kinda pricey…

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.880
Babe, everyone knows that time is money.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.579
So we’re actually saving money by not doing
chores ourselves!

00:00:24.579 --> 00:00:29.230
That’s why I’ve got Instacart delivering
our groceries, Rover walking the dog, Washio

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:32.110
doing our laundry and Taskrabbit cleaning
the gutters.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.400
Well, I guess that’ll give us more time
to write this episode.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.400
No need!

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.100
I already hired Writey-O to finish the episode
for us!

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.920
Wow… that’s really… meta.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.660
The convenience economy is nothing new.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:02.400
Ever since the industrial revolution, first-world
families have been willing to cough up hard-earned

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.920
dough to spend less time on household chores.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.820
"The confining hours around the washtub are a thing of the past."

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.820
Until recently, that meant gadgets and appliances,
but the internet has ushered in a new kind

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:19.180
of convenience: “on-demand” services that
quickly match clients with freelancers who

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.550
will do… just about anything.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:25.120
The on-demand industry is one of the fastest
growing in the world.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.799
Transportation and food delivery are still
the biggest sectors, but new services in areas

00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:33.070
like home and pet care are popping up all
the time.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:37.780
There’s even a service that matches you
with goats in your area who will come by and

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:38.780
trim your lawn!

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:39.780
Goats?

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.400
Oh, we’re totally doing that.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.900
It’s an economy driven not just by technology,
but changing social demographics.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:51.540
Not too long ago, it was standard for every
household to have one full-time employee who

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.710
took care of the vast majority of household
chores, and all for free!

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.070
(Thanks, Mom.)

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:01.420
But unpaid work is still work, and since more
women have entered the workforce, it makes

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.851
sense that we’re starting to assign dollar
values to the tasks that most of us got to

00:02:05.851 --> 00:02:06.851
take for granted.

00:02:06.851 --> 00:02:11.150
So how do you know whether you can afford
to outsource your chores?

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:16.330
When considering one of these on-demand services,
the first question you need to ask is whether

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:19.550
this is leverage or luxury.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.120
Leverage is more like an investment.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.760
Let’s say that by hiring a lawn service,
you save yourself two hours of yardwork, and

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.630
you invest that time into your side business
screenprinting T-shirts to sell on Etsy.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:34.489
Like the responsible side-hustler you are,
you’ve already calculated that you make

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:40.750
about $25 profit for every hour you put in,
and since the lawn service costs $40… congratulations!

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:44.380
You’ve successfully leveraged your money
to make more money.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.560
In other words, as long as you’re able to
make MORE per hour than what you’re paying

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.600
someone else, you’re coming out ahead.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.100
But let’s say that’s not an option for
you.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.489
Maybe you work 40 hours a week with no overtime
and no side hustle.

00:02:56.489 --> 00:03:01.080
Then you’re likely outsourcing chores for
a different reason, to free up personal time

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.569
to do stuff you actually like.

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:06.480
That puts this service into the luxury category.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.020
Luxuries are not necessarily bad things--but
they are harder to judge economically, because

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.660
you can’t really put a dollar value on “me”
time.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:18.459
But one thing’s for sure: no luxury is worth
going into debt over.

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:20.760
So that’s why you’ve got to “mind the
gap!”.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.519
If any outsourcing service pushes your monthly
expenses above your monthly income, you’ll

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:29.030
either have to forego it, or find somewhere
else to cut back.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:34.100
That means you’ll need a clear idea of how
much these services actually cost, and some

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.209
of them aren’t very transparent.

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:40.910
Grocery delivery services like Instacart are
clear about their delivery fees, but they

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:44.830
also charge hidden markups on many items that
are constantly fluctuating.

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:49.170
There’s no way to know how much extra you’re
paying without going down to the store to

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:52.430
price check, but that kinda defeats the purpose,
doesn’t it?

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:57.300
One instacart user actually did the math and
posted his results on reddit: turns out he

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:03.300
paid an average 42.8% markup, including some
items that cost well more than double their

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.710
in-store price.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:09.610
When you factor in the service and delivery
fees, he spent almost $70 to not have to go

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:10.610
to the supermarket.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:15.160
That might be on the high end of the spectrum,
but the point is: the easier these services

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.290
are to use, the less likely we are to keep
track of the costs.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:23.471
When you pay a cab driver in cash, you’re
very aware of the value of that ride.

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:26.560
But with Uber, you just get out of the car
and walk away.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:31.750
Depending on how you approach it, the on-demand
economy can be a solid investment, a path

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:35.880
to more freedom, or, if you’re not careful,
a financial ball and chain that could drag

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.650
you deeper into debt.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.790
We often recommend that saving for retirement
should take priority over present comfort,

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:46.470
because there will come a day when you really
won’t be able to mow your own lawn or shop

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:50.770
for your own groceries, and you’ll want
to be able to afford those services then.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:52.120
And that’s our two cents!

